---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import GuideLinks from '../../components/GuideLinks.astro';
import { guidesForTypeIndex } from '../../lib/guide-picks';
import { TYPES, inType, typeUrl, ratedLines, share, medianOf } from '../../lib/taxonomy';
import { fmt } from '../../lib/data';
const rows = TYPES.map((t) => ({ t, ls: inType(t) }));
const marked = new Set(rows.flatMap((r) => r.ls.map((l) => `${l.brand_slug}/${l.line_slug}`)));
const unmarked = ratedLines.length - marked.size;
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'By category', href: '/type/' }];
const ld = [{ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'ItemList', name: 'Tires by category', itemListElement: rows.map((r, i) => ({ '@type': 'ListItem', position: i + 1, name: r.t.label, url: new URL(typeUrl(r.t), Astro.site).href })) }];
---
<Base title="Tires by Category: All-Season, Touring, Performance, All-Terrain, Highway" description="Browse NHTSA-rated tire lines by category, using only the markers in the product name (A/S, Touring, HP, A/T, H/T). Median treadwear and grade mixes per category." crumbs={crumbs} ld={ld} og="type/index">
  <h1>Tires by category</h1>
  <div class="answer"><strong>How this works:</strong> NHTSA’s UTQG data has no “tire type” field. We sort lines into a category only when the product name says so (for example “A/S”, “Touring”, “HP”, “A/T”, “H/T”). The label is incomplete: most lines carry no marker, and a line without one is not “not all-season”. Winter tires are outside UTQG, so there is no winter category (<a href="/guides/all-season-vs-all-weather-vs-winter-tires/">why</a>).</div>
  <h2>How a name becomes a category</h2>
  <p>The ratings file has columns for treadwear, traction, and temperature. It does not have a column for all-season, touring, performance, all-terrain, or highway-terrain. This page adds a label only by matching the product name NHTSA filed. The markers in use are {rows.map((r) => r.t.markers).join('; ')}. A line whose name matches two markers is counted in both rows of the table, so the line counts can add up to more than the number of distinct lines. {marked.size.toLocaleString()} of {ratedLines.length.toLocaleString()} rated lines match at least one marker. The other {unmarked.toLocaleString()} rated lines stay off every category. They are unlabeled, not placed in a leftover type, and a missing marker is not a finding that the tire is something else.</p>
  <p>Winter tires are outside UTQG, so there is no winter row to open. The <a href="/guides/all-season-vs-all-weather-vs-winter-tires/">season guide</a> explains that exemption. The <a href="/guides/all-terrain-vs-highway-terrain-tires/">all-terrain versus highway-terrain guide</a> reads the A/T and H/T name groups against the grades in the file, and it keeps a maker’s mileage warranty separate from the median.</p>
  <h2>How to use a category page</h2>
  <p>Open a category for the lines whose names match, the brands that file the most of those names, and the mix of traction and temperature grades. The median on that page is the median of those lines’ own median grades. It describes the names in this file. It is not the grade of the tire on your car, and it is not a mileage promise. For a size you drive, open the <a href="/sizes/">size list</a> and read the grade NHTSA filed for that size. Lines with no marker still appear on size pages and on the <a href="/treadwear/">treadwear bands</a>, which group every rated line by its median grade.</p>
  <div class="tw"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Name markers</th><th class="n">Lines</th><th class="n">Share of rated lines</th><th class="n">Median treadwear</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    {rows.map((r) => <tr><td><a href={typeUrl(r.t)}>{r.t.label}</a></td><td>{r.t.markers}</td><td class="n">{r.ls.length}</td><td class="n">{share(r.ls.length, ratedLines.length)}%</td><td class="n">{fmt(medianOf(r.ls))}</td></tr>)}
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p class="tbl-note">A line can match more than one marker. Out of {ratedLines.length.toLocaleString()} rated lines.</p>
  <div class="cardgrid">{rows.map((r) => <a class="gcard" href={typeUrl(r.t)}><b>{r.t.label}</b><span>{r.t.blurb}</span><small>{r.ls.length} lines · median treadwear {fmt(medianOf(r.ls))}</small></a>)}</div>
  <GuideLinks slugs={guidesForTypeIndex()} />
</Base>
